#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119627 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119816 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6cba14b5-62f7-5fbe-beb1-9b1511305cc6" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Basis of the Null Space of a Matrix Given a Row Equivalent Matrix"}] :question/id #uuid "1c4e02bb-5ec0-572e-92ba-46d16c6f03fa" :question/math-academy-id "e-4819" :question/problem "The matrices $A$ and $B$ are given below. If $A$ is row equivalent to $B$, then find a basis of $\\textrm{Null}(A).$\n\n![](images/51/517c8b69f022d892daa4f8f48a74f6a93b12143e71f5077a046133b1b396ee9f.svg)" :question/worked-solution "To find a basis of $\\textrm{Null}(A),$ we need to solve the equation $A\\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{0}$ and express the solution as a linear combination of linearly independent vectors. Those vectors form a basis of $\\textrm{Null}(A).$\n\nSince $A\\sim B,$ the equation $A \\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{0}$ is equivalent to the equation $B\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0},$ which corresponds to the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}=0 \\\\ {x}_{2}-\\frac{1}{3}{x}_{3}+\\frac{2}{3}{x}_{4}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the general solution is $x_1=0$ and $x_2=\\dfrac{1}{3}x_3 - \\dfrac{2}{3} x_4$ where $x_3$ and $x_4$ are free variables. Therefore, if $\\mathbf{x}\\in\\textrm{Null}(A),$ then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{4}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}{x}_{3}-\\frac{2}{3}{x}_{4} \\\\ {x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{4}\\end{aligned}]={x}_{3}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{4}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3},{x}_{4}\\in R.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis implies that\n\n![](images/50/50bf947d3beddfb5be8eee27c0d77bf57f3e93204a8554ffffa1e4cc41f35956.svg)\n\nis a basis of $\\textrm{Null}(A)$ since it is a linearly independent set and its vectors generate $\\textrm{Null}(A).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119628 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119817 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3e66c269-233b-57f8-9dae-46eb78c228bc" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Basis for a Given Null Space"}] :question/id #uuid "1cbcbca0-c8b5-55c9-af93-9b9c0ba59ffb" :question/math-academy-id "e-4820" :question/problem "Given that  ![](images/b4/b489811024ece2341b91e0b9819714a988cf31a87598601a8a1257c02129698c.svg) find a basis for $\\textrm{Null}(A).$" :question/worked-solution "Any vector in $\\textrm{Null}(A)$ has the form\n\n![](images/94/94af7adea734ab0f38b5f8521fcf575cbc263cc1700a3144f682706711604859.svg)\n\nExpanding, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}3{x}_{3}-{x}_{4} \\\\ 4{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{4}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}3{x}_{3} \\\\ 4{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{4} \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ {x}_{4}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={x}_{3}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{4}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, a basis for $\\textrm{Null}(A)$ is\n\n![](images/4a/4a8f749660a2c8b71cd9fe61c97180fd143a3c06c754e75a1dc51c14622f03a2.svg)\n\nsince it is a linearly independent set and its vectors generate $\\textrm{Null}(A).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119629 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119818 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5c70963f-589d-532b-9286-559f68693df7" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Basis for the Null Space of a Matrix Given in Reduced Row Echelon Form"}] :question/id #uuid "a9d2ed82-2ba5-53a6-973d-c859ea5c295f" :question/math-academy-id "e-4821" :question/problem "Find a basis of the null space of the matrix ![](images/35/35651f90fba8141cafc7d513670e9903a4977a2c4bd3a8317ee7be90f4e7c493.svg)" :question/worked-solution "To find a basis of $\\textrm{Null}(A),$ we need to solve the equation $A\\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{0}$ and express the solution as a linear combination of linearly independent vectors. Those vectors form a basis of $\\textrm{Null}(A).$\n\nThe given matrix is already in reduced row echelon form. So, the equation $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ corresponds to the following system:\n![](images/af/afdd7aba38057c3f4b226c63d103c83d3a76171ae8ae2a900db4c875a9c4e023.svg)\n\nHere, $x_1$ and $x_3$ are basic variables corresponding to the pivot columns ($1$st and $3$rd) and $x_2,x_4$ are free variables corresponding to the non-pivot columns. Therefore, if $\\mathbf{x} \\in \\textrm{Null}(A),$ then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{4}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-3{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ 4{x}_{4} \\\\ {x}_{4}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 4{x}_{4} \\\\ {x}_{4}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{4}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $x_2,x_4\\in\\mathbb{R}.$ This implies that\n\n![](images/4d/4d430991c440bc6e0afad527930566f4c839476f8f1c4f1ee91f2f726d0ece27.svg)\n\nis a basis of $\\textrm{Null}(A)$ since it is a linearly independent set and its vectors generate $\\textrm{Null}(A).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119630 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119819 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0fd5a675-c692-505e-900b-d7500fb7c653" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Basis for the Null Space of a Matrix That Is Not in Reduced Row Echelon Form"}] :question/id #uuid "ef3be538-d974-5371-bd5b-a7e2ef3df827" :question/math-academy-id "e-4822" :question/problem "Given the matrix ![](images/ec/ec919391ecd75553fc12f04d1cb6832c7019677ff0954eaffded42fd2cdb8cc2.svg) find a basis of $\\textrm{Null}(A).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we reduce the matrix $A$ to reduced row echelon form (RREF):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 5\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-2){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-2){R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equation $A\\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{0}$ is equivalent to the system\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}=0 \\\\ {x}_{2}=0 \\\\ {x}_{3}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis implies that the only solution of $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ is $\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}.$ So $\\textrm{Null}(A)=\\{ \\mathbf{0} \\},$ which means that the basis of $\\textrm{Null}(A)$ is the empty set, i.e., $\\mathcal{B}=\\{\\,\\}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186398053 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119817 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3e66c269-233b-57f8-9dae-46eb78c228bc" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Basis for a Given Null Space"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bfcffbe5-1391-58fa-a758-12ee97cbeaef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B={[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398055} {:answer/id #uuid "81092aae-3b21-5088-85a1-58d891002d69" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B={[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398056} {:answer/id #uuid "76c389fc-2968-58e5-88a2-67b42fb29a93" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B={[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ \\,\\,1 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398057} {:answer/id #uuid "bc6bbfd9-40b7-5c44-af28-56d554c70bec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B={[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398058} {:answer/id #uuid "18530549-03b9-564b-8a59-91912e430d11" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B={[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398059}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "76c389fc-2968-58e5-88a2-67b42fb29a93" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B={[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ \\,\\,1 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398057} :answer-field/id #uuid "d97f34e8-16f1-5737-9558-b546535981e2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398054}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7e63214c-016a-55f5-bcc6-73d6df35ee23" :question/math-academy-id "q-111349" :question/problem "Given that  $\\text{Null}(A)={[\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ -3{x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]\\,:\\,{x}_{2}\\in R}$, find a basis for $\\text{Null}(A).$" :question/worked-solution "Any vector in $\\text{Null}(A)$ has the form\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ -3{x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nExpanding, we get \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ -3{x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]={x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ \\,\\,1 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, a basis for $\\text{Null}(A)$ is\n\n$$\nB={[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ \\,\\,1 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]}\n$$\n\nsince it is a linearly independent set that generates $\\text{Null}(A).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247226 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119819 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0fd5a675-c692-505e-900b-d7500fb7c653" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Basis for the Null Space of a Matrix That Is Not in Reduced Row Echelon Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "25762eb3-d3a8-53b9-8de5-e9bee2598b4c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B={[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244647} {:answer/id #uuid "7e915c1d-b54b-574b-8895-f85df62d8267" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B={[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245432} {:answer/id #uuid "8c6aef69-71f8-5aee-be03-ea9a486bb6fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B={\\,}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245545} {:answer/id #uuid "98391602-2050-56e8-9792-fe9d5fa56d0b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B={[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245663} {:answer/id #uuid "be697e31-fad2-56d4-bb45-56c43ee29a33" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B={[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246011}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "25762eb3-d3a8-53b9-8de5-e9bee2598b4c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B={[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244647} :answer-field/id #uuid "d045bd23-b203-58ca-9409-b1fd70b89335" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246921}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "dc0b0ced-e72a-5ee0-ae93-04a4f0845d7c" :question/math-academy-id "q-111509" :question/problem "Given the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -1 & 2\\end{aligned}]$, find a basis of $\\text{Null}(A).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we reduce the matrix $A$ to reduced row echelon form (RREF):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -1 & 2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+4{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :=(-1){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-2){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equation $Ax=0$ is equivalent to the system\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ {x}_{3}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe general solution is ${x}_{1}=-{x}_{2}$ and ${x}_{3}=0,$ where ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. Therefore, if $x\\in \\text{Null}(A),$ then\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]={x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nThis implies that \n\n$$\nB={[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}\n$$\n\nis a basis of $\\text{Null}(A)$ since it is a linearly independent set and its vectors generate $\\text{Null}(A).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395825 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119819 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0fd5a675-c692-505e-900b-d7500fb7c653" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Basis for the Null Space of a Matrix That Is Not in Reduced Row Echelon Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f7d068a8-8c16-5895-a8a0-6d5c967450ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B={[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395827} {:answer/id #uuid "6cdaec59-e1c5-5eb0-a903-7aa07bfa40a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B={[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395828} {:answer/id #uuid "4f9a7859-76ff-5f7c-b9ba-abb5dc1b6768" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B={[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395829} {:answer/id #uuid "b21e78e2-0491-596e-bd15-82fb1e997dcd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B={[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395830} {:answer/id #uuid "c6628db1-d645-5914-9bc3-a35c7a222729" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B={[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395831}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b21e78e2-0491-596e-bd15-82fb1e997dcd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B={[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395830} :answer-field/id #uuid "eb2dd0d4-9bed-58b4-940b-d9ecf1e1866c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395826}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4b9db0d1-0644-55bd-9fa5-174e2a1643f2" :question/math-academy-id "q-34422" :question/problem "Given the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 9 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 6 & 3\\end{aligned}]$, find a basis of $\\text{Null}(A).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we reduce the matrix $A$ to reduced row echelon form (RREF):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 9 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 6 & 3\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-4){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 6 & 3\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-2){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equation $Ax=0$ is equivalent to the system\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ {x}_{2}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe general solution is ${x}_{1}=-{x}_{3}$ and ${x}_{2}=0,$ where ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. Therefore, if $x\\in \\text{Null}(A),$ then\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{3} \\\\ 0 \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]={x}_{3}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nThis implies that \n\n$$\nB={[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}\n$$\n\nis a basis of $\\text{Null}(A)$ since it is a linearly independent set and its vectors generate $\\text{Null}(A).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120343 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119816 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6cba14b5-62f7-5fbe-beb1-9b1511305cc6" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Basis of the Null Space of a Matrix Given a Row Equivalent Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0f7bd4f8-f387-5785-8a86-397fec0c58e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B={[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317618} {:answer/id #uuid "1787b2d2-3f1c-50c6-bd21-32156b18b5b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B={[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317639} {:answer/id #uuid "c643dca0-aa47-583c-bee4-e5e11975701c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B={[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318218} {:answer/id #uuid "d81e6138-f406-53fc-ada0-fe5740f9e603" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B={\\,}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318283} {:answer/id #uuid "ed510e7f-4cc8-503e-af7c-48859c66d232" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B={[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318359}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1787b2d2-3f1c-50c6-bd21-32156b18b5b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B={[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317639} :answer-field/id #uuid "709394e6-219b-5a42-ac2b-888d3c374e30" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318666}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7788836f-aadb-5c09-b706-0eb988c022c6" :question/math-academy-id "q-34522" :question/problem "The matrices $A$ and $B$ are given below. Given that $A$ is row equivalent to $B$, then find a basis of $\\text{Null}(A).$\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -5 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To find a basis of $\\text{Null}(A),$ we need to solve the equation $Ax=0$ and express the solution as a linear combination of linearly independent vectors. Those vectors form a basis of $\\text{Null}(A).$\n\nSince $A∼B,$ the equation $Ax=0$ is equivalent to the equation $Bx=0,$ which corresponds to the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ {x}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the general solution is ${x}_{1}=-{x}_{3}$ and ${x}_{2}=0,$ where ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. Therefore, if $x\\in \\text{Null}(A),$ then\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{3} \\\\ 0 \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]={x}_{3}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nThis implies that \n\n$$\nB={[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}\n$$\n\nis a basis of $\\text{Null}(A)$ since it is a linearly independent set and its vectors generate $\\text{Null}(A).$"}]}
